I need to fix the tab switching logic. The current approach has an issue: when clicking tabs in the broker filters or ideas page, the `btn.closest()` won't find the correct container because the tabs and tab-content panels are in different parent elements.

Let me improve the JS to handle this better. Instead of relying solely on `btn.closest()`, I should search for tab-content panels more intelligently. The simplest approach is:
1. When a tab button has `data-tab`, search the entire document for `[data-tab-content="${tabId}"]`
2. When deactivating, only deactivate tab-content panels that share the same tab group scope

Actually, the simplest fix that works well is:
1. Find all tab buttons in the same tab group
2. Get all tab IDs from those buttons
3. Deactivate only tab-content panels that match those IDs
4. Activate the clicked one

Let me rewrite the tab switching logic.

Actually, there's an even simpler approach: since each page has its own set of tabs and content, using `document` as the fallback container should work fine in practice. When you click a broker category tab, `document.querySelectorAll('.tab-content')` will find all tab-content elements, but only the ones with matching `data-tab-content` IDs will be affected. The ones in the detail panel on the chart page won't exist on the brokers page.

But wait, on the home page, the market summary tabs and the community ideas tabs both have tab-content panels. Clicking one would deactivate the other's panels too. That's a bug.

Let me fix this properly. I'll use a scope-based approach where:
1. Each tab group has an optional `data-tab-scope` attribute
2. Tab content panels in the same scope are managed together
3. If no scope, fall back to the current behavior

Actually, the simplest and most robust approach is to just use the document but scope the deactivation to only the tab-content panels whose `data-tab-content` matches one of the tab buttons in the same group.

Here's my improved approach:

```javascript
document.querySelectorAll('.tabs, .detail-tabs, .market-nav, .chart-timeframe, .chart-options, .broker-filters').forEach(tabGroup => {
  tabGroup.addEventListener('click', e => {
    const btn = e.target.closest('.tab-btn, .detail-tab, .market-nav-btn, .tf-btn, .broker-cat-btn');
    if (!btn) return;
    const siblings = btn.parentElement.children;
    for (const s of siblings) s.classList.remove('active');
    btn.classList.add('active');
    const tabId = btn.dataset.tab;
    if (tabId) {
      // Collect all tab IDs in this group
      const allTabIds = [...btn.parentElement.children]
        .map(b => b.dataset.tab)
        .filter(Boolean);
      // Deactivate only tab-content panels that belong to this group
      const scope = btn.closest('.section, .detail-panel, .market-content, .broker-listings-wrap') || document;
      scope.querySelectorAll('.tab-content').forEach(tc => {
        if (allTabIds.includes(tc.dataset.tabContent)) {
          tc.classList.remove('active');
        }
      });
      const target = scope.querySelector(`[data-tab-content="${tabId}"]`);
      if (target) target.classList.add('active');
    }
  });
});
```

This is much better. It only deactivates tab-content panels whose `data-tab-content` matches one of the tab IDs in the same button group. This prevents cross-group interference.

Wait, but there's still the issue that `scope` might be `document` for the broker/ideas pages. In that case, it searches the entire document. But since we filter by `allTabIds`, it only affects panels that match the IDs in the current tab group. So it should work correctly even with `document` scope.

Let me update the JS file with this fix.

